{"gid":"2026_05_HOU_TEN","week":5,"away":"HOU","home":"TEN","away_col":"#F2405A","home_col":"#4FC3E8","spread":-3.5,"total":43.5,"fav":"HOU","hpts":20,"apts":24,"kickoff":"Sun Oct 11 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Gunnar Helm</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 HOU are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.34 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>The <strong>TEN</strong> welcome the <strong>HOU</strong> as underdogs of <strong>3.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>43.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>HOU 24\u201320</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>33% of its snaps with HOU in front</strong>, <strong>42% within one score</strong>, and <strong>25% with HOU chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>A <strong>modest edge to HOU</strong>. They should lead more often than not, tilting them slightly toward the run late, while TEN leans a touch more pass-heavy to stay in it \u2014 but the effect is gentle; this isn't a script that dramatically reshapes anyone's usage.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Tony Pollard</strong> \u2014 HOU are stingy against the run (100 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Kayshon Boutte</strong> (WR2) \u2014 TEN leak 44 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Gunnar Helm</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 HOU are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.34 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Nico Collins</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 TEN surrender 0.53 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to HOU \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Woody Marks</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>HOU \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>HOU are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Woody Marks</strong> (21 carries, 80 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though HOU's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Nico Collins</strong> projects for <strong>10 targets and 95 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Dalton Schultz</strong> (TE, 7 targets, 48 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>TEN \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>TEN are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>38%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 TEN project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tony Pollard</strong>'s rushing projection (21 carries, 95 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, TEN funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 67 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Gunnar Helm</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 32 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 4-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"HOU","col":"#F2405A","players":[{"name":"C.J. Stroud","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":244.75,"book":null,"over_th":188.59,"under_th":274.77,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.44,"book":null,"over_th":0.95,"under_th":1.65,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":7.3,"fair":"14/1","book":null,"value":null},"d":{"pass_yards":{"m":244.75,"q":[0.0,28.46,228.63,459.33,673.41],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"pass_td":{"m":1.44,"q":[0.0,0.0,1.19,3.51,5.82],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"rush_yards":{"m":7.98,"q":[0.0,0.0,4.57,14.71,24.85],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"rush_att":{"m":1.79,"q":[0.0,0.0,1.01,3.08,4.11],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null}}},{"name":"David Montgomery","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"